morning victims have waited decades for  this day to see a former prime minister  questioned over the government's  response to the infected blood scandal  the evidence i shall give shall be the  truth shall be the truth the whole truth  the whole truth  but far from any apology or admission of  guilt there was instead a comment seen  as offensive sir john major describing  what happened as bad luck  there's no amount of compensation you  can give that could actually compensate  for what had happened to them what had  happened to them was  incredibly  bad luck  many clearly disagree  and and the scandal saw up to 5 000 nhs  patients infected with hiv or hepatitis  c after being given contaminated blood  products in the 70s and 80s around 3000  of whom have since died  among them is nikki calder infected with  hiv before he was 10 during treatment  for hemophilia he died aged 25. i try  not to be angry  and i try not to be bitter  it was very difficult  but rosemary says that anger is much  harder to suppress when listening to  comments such as sir john's  bad luck is when you trip over a paving  stone and break your leg or something  bad luck isn't when you go when you say  to somebody oh bad luck you've been  given a a potentially life-threatening  virus  that's not bad luck  we want people to just stand up and say  we did wrong  no government has taken responsibility  for the scandal and it was the same  again today sir john major defending his  roles in margaret thatcher's cabinets  and his own and the refusal to award  compensation  everyone in this room can think of a  dozen things where you could argue  someone has a moral obligation to do  something  and  the government has to keep control of  events  and keep control of the sum total  expenditure  throughout his evidence sir john major  was at pains to say the government was  not unsympathetic to victims and that  the decision against compensation was  purely practical to avoid setting a  potentially costly 